	"descriptionMarkdown": "A book of modular station blueprints. \nSet down the Network brain, adjust the values to match your train lengths per world, it is defaulted to mine.\nSet down the depots for both fluid and item trains, hook the stations via the green wire so they can receive the signals.\nSet down a provider station, pick the resource it provides then place the appropriate loader (item/fluid). Make it as long as your train is.\nSet down a request station, pick the resource it wants then place the appropriate unloader (item/fluid). Make it as long as you train.\n\nOnce everything is down, and the rails are connected, Place down the locomotives and assemble them to your desired length. Once fuelled and set to Automatic you have an automated rail network.\n\nDepots are only for holding trains and telling them where to go. They are split between Fluid and Items otherwise a Fluid train will try to go pick up from a item provider (and vice versa). When it receives a resource signal over the green wire, it will head to the provider with that name that is active.\n\nProviders only activate once there is enough resource in each section to fully fill a wagon and all sections pass that criteria. Trains will wait here until they receive the signal that a Requester station of it's type is available.\n\nRequesters only activate if it has enough space in each of it's wagon unloaders to take a fully loaded train. Once the Train is empty it will return to the depot and await the next signal for a Provider\n\nBoth Providers and Requesters, once a train is on the way, will remove it's signal that it can provide/request so as to not bring more trains that it can handle. If you do not have a queue for your stations, leave the '*' signal on the brain to 1 and only a single train will be sent at a time. Increase that count for however big your queues are to have multiple trains go to the same station, it will still only request the amount of trains that it can actually fill.",
